 "offset": 2525.0,
 "text": "Thank you, Steve. Now Frank, I know you haven't gone. It's the first thing on your backlist to get there. But I'd like you to share with us, certainly in the work that you're doing with Gillian Segel at the moment, maybe before. What was a defining moment for you that got you to think in terms of your work, just how important Israel was and the Jewish community is. Just go closer, Hotel. And what anti-Semitism was. We talk about hatred in our communities and that we should stamp out all hatred. And for me, that clarity came when I realized that anti-Semitism was not like every other hatred. It was unique and it was shape-shifting in nature. We obviously saw it started out as a hatred for a religion, and then last century we saw a shift to a hatred for a race and a people, and in this century is a hate for the nation state of Israel. And that clarity led me to realise that the things that we see in our community when I took a dusk of Julian a few days after, or when Bona happened, the reason that is happening, and it's only happening to one community, is because anti-7 is unique and it is a different hatred. It is not the same as every other hatred that we now see in our communities. So there's a well-known saying that says first they come to the Saturday people, and then they come to the Sunday people, warning that anti-Semitism is really something which",
